{"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cg2GJH8fqp4JYCGYxGh2r", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}, {"type": "text", "text": "# Lesson: Choosing an output\n\n**The skill:** Name the specific thing you want when the model finishes. Pick that thing based on how you'll use it.\n\n## Same request, three ways\n\n**1. Topic only**\n> Help me with packing for a weekend trip.\n\nThis names no output. You might get advice, a story, or questions back.\n\n**2. A category, but the shape is open**\n> Make a packing checklist for a weekend trip.\n\nThis is better. But you could still get 5 items or 40, grouped or ungrouped.\n\n**3. A clear output, said briefly**\n> Write a three-item checklist of essentials for a rainy weekend hike, one item per line, each starting with \u2610.\n\nAnyone reading only this prompt could picture the result:\n\n```\n\u2610 Rain jacket\n\u2610 Water bottle\n\u2610 Phone charger\n```\n\n## Why a checklist?\n\nChoose the format by what you'll **do** with the output:\n\n- **Tick things off** \u2192 checklist\n- **Choose between options** \u2192 comparison table\n- **Understand something** \u2192 short explanation\n\nYou'll use a packing list while packing, so a checklist fits better than a paragraph of advice.\n\n## Quick test\n\nAsk yourself: *Could two careful people give me different things from this prompt?* If yes, describe the output more clearly.\n\nDon't over-correct, though. Only add words that make the output clearer. A short prompt that already names the output is a good prompt.\n\n---\n\n**Next (optional), just reply with your choice:**\n- **Practice:** Write your own prompt for a different list, such as groceries or a meeting prep list. Also tell me separately what you expect the output to look like. Then we'll look at what you chose.\n- **Later:** We stop here.\n\n**About your Learning Card:** Reading this lesson doesn't show independent skill, so I won't record it as